<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_4_1">


<title>Algebra of Powers: Integral Exponents</title>

<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
            State the definitions of <m>a^p</m> for zero, positive integer, and negative integer exponents <m>p</m>.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
        Apply the Rules of Exponents to rewrite and simplify expressions involving integer exponents as a single power.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
            Recognize which operations on power expressions, such as distributing an exponent over a sum, are not valid.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</objectives>


<p>
In the next two sections we review algebra of power expressions of the form: <md>a^p</md> where <m>a</m> and <m>p</m> are given numbers. The number <m>a</m> is called the <em>base</em> in the expression and the power <m>p</m> that the base is being raised to is called the <em>exponent</em>.
</p>

<p>
We begin with integral exponents; that is, exponents that are integers. If <m>p</m> is a positive integer <m>p = 1, 2, 3, \dots</m>, then <m>a^p</m> is a short way of writing repeated multiplication:
<md>
a^p = \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p \text{ copies of } a}</md>
In particular, for <m>p = 1, 2, 3\dots</m>,
<md>
0^p = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad 1^p = 1.
</md>
</p>

<p>
This definition can be expanded to the exponent <m>p = 0</m> by defining for any number <m>a</m>,
<md>
a^0 = 1.
</md>
Note that, by definition, this means
<md>
0^0 = 1.
</md>
We can also extend this definition to negative integer exponents by defining for every <m>a \neq 0</m> and every <m>p = 0, 1, 2, 3, \dots</m>,
<md>
a^{-p} = \frac{1}{a^p}.
</md>
In particular: <md>a^{-1} = \frac{1}{a}</md>
</p>

<p>
These definitions lead to the basic properties of power expressions.
</p>

<theorem>
<title>Rules of Exponents <mdash/> Integral Exponents</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be given numbers, and let <m>p</m> and <m>r</m> be integers. Then the following equalities hold provided both sides are defined:
</p>

<p><ol>
<li xml:id="int-exps-prop1">
    <m>\displaystyle a^0 = 1</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop2">
    <m>\displaystyle a^{-p} = \frac{1}{a^p}</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop3">
    <m>\displaystyle a^p \cdot a^r = a^{p + r}</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop4">
    <m>\displaystyle \frac{a^p}{a^r} = a^{p - r}</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop5">
    <m>\displaystyle (a^p)^r = a^{p \cdot r}</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop6">
    <m>\displaystyle (a \cdot b)^p = a^p \cdot b^p</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop7">
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{a}{b} \right)^p = \frac{a^p}{b^p}</m>
</li>

<li xml:id="int-exps-prop8">
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{a}{b} \right)^{-p} = \left( \frac{b}{a} \right)^{p}</m>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Note that an expression in the formulas above may not be defined if there is a zero in the denominator. For example, in <xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>, <m>\dfrac{1}{a^p}</m> is not defined if <m>a = 0</m> and <m>p \neq 0</m>. That means that <xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref> holds for any <m>a \neq 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
The properties of exponents listed above follow from the definitions at the beginning of this section.
This is illustrated for <xref ref="int-exps-prop3">Property</xref> below:
<md>
a^p \cdot a^r =
\underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p \text{ copies}}
\cdot \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{r \text{ copies}}
= \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p + r  \text{ copies}}
= a^{p + r}.</md>
</p>

<example>
<statement>
<p>
    Rewrite each expression given below as a power of 3; that is, in the form <m>3^p</m> for some constant <m>p</m>.
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{3^2}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle 3^5 \cdot 3^{-2}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \frac{(3^2)^3}{3^8}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{3^{-3}}{3^{4}} \right)^2</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle \left( \frac{3^5}{27} \right)^2</m>
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    By <xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>: <md>\frac{1}{3^2} = 3^{-2}.</md> We rewrote the expression as <m>3^p</m> for <m>p = -2</m>.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    By <xref ref="int-exps-prop3">Property</xref>: <md>3^5 \cdot 3^{-2} = 3^{(5 + (-2))} = 3^{3}.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    By <xref ref="int-exps-prop5">Property</xref> and <xref ref="int-exps-prop4">Property</xref>: <md>\frac{(3^2)^3}{3^8} = \frac{3^6}{3^8} = 3^{(6 - 8)} = 3^{-2}.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    As <m>\displaystyle 3^{-3} = \frac{1}{3^3}</m> we have:
<md>
<mrow>
\left( \frac{3^{-3}}{3^{4}} \right)^2
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>}{=}
\left( \frac{1}{3^3 \cdot 3^{4}} \right)^2
\amp
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop3">Property</xref>}{=}
\left( \frac{1}{3^7} \right)^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>}{=}
(3^{-7})^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop5">Property</xref>}{=}
3^{-14}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    Note that <m>27 = 3^3</m>. Using <xref ref="int-exps-prop7">Property</xref>: <md>\left( \frac{3^5}{27} \right)^2 = \left( \frac{3^5}{3^3} \right)^2 = \frac{3^{10}}{3^6} = 3^{4}.</md>
</p>
</li>

</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example>
<statement>
<p>
    Use Rules of Exponents to simplify the following expressions if possible:
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle (x^2 \cdot x^3)^2</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\left( \dfrac{x^2 y^5}{x^4} \right)^{-3}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\left( \dfrac{x^{-2} y^5}{x^{-3} y^2} \right)^{-1}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>(a + b)^7</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
Use <xref ref="int-exps-prop3">Property</xref> to simplify the expression under the outside exponent <m>2</m>, then use <xref ref="int-exps-prop5">Property</xref>:
<md>(x^2 \cdot x^3)^2 = (x^5)^2 = x^{10}.</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Begin by simplifying the expression under the exponent:
<md>
<mrow>
\left( \frac{x^2 y^5}{x^4} \right)^{-3}
\amp \overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop4">Property</xref>}{=}
\left( \frac{y^5}{x^2} \right)^{-3}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop8">Property</xref>}{=}
\left( \frac{x^2}{y^5} \right)^{3}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop7">Property</xref>}{=}
\frac{(x^2)^3}{(y^5)^3} = \frac{x^6}{y^{15}}
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Again, begin by simplifying under the exponent <m>-1</m>:
<md>
\left( \frac{x^{-2} y^5}{x^{-3} y^2} \right)^{-1}
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>}{=}
\left( \frac{x^{3} y^5}{x^{2} y^2} \right)^{-1}
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop4">Property</xref>}{=}
(x y^3)^{-1}
\overset{<xref ref="int-exps-prop2">Property</xref>}{=}
\frac{1}{x y^3}
.
</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    There is no rule for the power of a sum or a difference! We cannot simplify <m>(a + b)^7</m> using rules of exponents. You certainly <term>cannot</term> distribute the power <m>7</m> and write the expression as <m>a^7 + b^7</m>.
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</solution>
</example>




<xi:include href="exercises/ex_4_1.ptx"/>

</section>